WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
Offcut turns usable stock into a
cutting plan that people and browser

2
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:09.280
agents can work on together. Here,
all fourteen requested parts fit,

3
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:15.280
and the long board stays protected. This
workshop is an original illustrative example.

4
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:21.220
Enter your own usable lengths, finished parts,
blade kerf, and reusable-remnant threshold.

5
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.840
People own these measurements and protection controls;
agents cannot change them through the registered

6
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.400
tools. The right-hand panel is a
separate recording harness, not a chatbot

7
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.700
inside the website. A real model chooses native Web M C P calls.

8
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:44.860
It reads the live revision, stock and requirements
without reconstructing the page from pixels.

9
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:51.640
The model requests three complete alternatives:
use less stock, handle fewer boards,

10
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:57.460
or keep useful offcuts. The website's
deterministic branch-and-bound solver checks every

11
00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:02.820
part quantity, saw pass and stock length.
Search limits are reported honestly,

12
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:09.320
not as invented optimality. Both efficient-handling
options open seven point two metres

13
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:14.700
across three stock pieces, with two
hundred millimetres of waste. Keeping

14
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.720
useful offcuts opens ten point zero
eight metres across six pieces,

15
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.220
leaving forty-two millimetres of waste and
three point zero three eight metres reusable.

16
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.960
That avoids one hundred fifty-eight millimetres
of short scrap, but handles more stock.

17
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.440
Nothing here claims carbon savings.
The agent inspects the exact least-stock plan

18
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.400
and stages it for review. Using and
Protected identify the real stock records.

19
00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:48.000
The registered tool surface has no approval
operation. The acknowledgement and approval happen

20
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.780
in the website UI; these recording controls
are automated, not proof of a physical human.

21
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.860
After that separate approval, the model
inspects the release and exports its actual CSV.

22
00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.860
The download contains all fourteen parts,
exact offsets, kerf and the approved plan

23
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:11.500
identifier. A different selected draft
cannot silently replace it. Now the blade

24
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.940
kerf changes from three to four millimetres.
The old approval is invalidated.

25
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.260
The model reads the new revision,
inspects the stale plan and generates

26
00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:26.420
a fresh proposal with fifty-six millimetres
of sawdust. It stages another review,

27
00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:32.400
without approving. Offcut runs without an
application account, model key or backend.

28
00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.900
The live workbench and MIT-licensed source
are public. These are planning estimates,

29
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.020
not machine instructions: verify usable stock
and safe cutting conditions at the bench.

